          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-9299dcd45fdf\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Christian's direct “Je t'aime !” primarily avows as true his loving feeling toward Roxane. Although uttered in a courtship scene, it does not independently undertake a commitment or obligation. The exclamation mark and emotional situation do not establish expressive/reflexive force, and nothing important in the core utterance falls outside T/P/E.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The declaration pragmatically advances Christian's courtship, but courtship and desired relational effect alone do not supply performative undertaking force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Christian directly addresses the love declaration to Roxane within the dramatic action.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"In the wider balcony-scene context, Christian attempts to woo Roxane in his own words rather than relying on Cyrano's eloquence. His blunt, repetitive declaration is sincere as an avowal but fails to satisfy Roxane's demand for elaborated language. That dramatic inadequacy concerns rhetorical execution, not a core force beyond truth-conditional avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev-local-1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“CHRISTIAN: Je t'aime !”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person declaration, assigned directly to Christian, strongly supports truth-conditional avowal and direct utterance status.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev-meta-1\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The supplied metadata identifies the source as Edmond Rostand's French play Cyrano de Bergerac.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the speaker label and line constitute staged dramatic dialogue rather than narrator report.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev-bg-1\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In the surrounding balcony episode, Christian tries to speak to Roxane without Cyrano's verbal assistance; Roxane finds his bare declarations rhetorically insufficient and asks for greater elaboration.\",\"supports\":\"Reinforces that the line functions as a direct, blunt avowal in courtship rather than as a commitment or reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.93}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"extensive\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"contribution\":\"Identified the line's place in the balcony courtship episode and the contrast between Christian's sincere but ineloquent avowal and Roxane's demand for eloquence.\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E/O naturally represents the core utterance as a strong truth-conditional avowal. Its courtship purpose and rhetorical inadequacy are adequately handled as context, without P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
